A||||11||sluppy123||engine swap
Z||000000||sluppy123||07-26-99||06:52 PM||||I just bought a 87 Grand AM this weekend for $100 It has the Tech 4 engine in it with a five speed. My question is if I rebuild the engine and put it in my 85 fiero will it bolt up to my old 3-speed auto?
Z||000001||PaUL||07-26-99||07:33 PM||||That 2.5 Tech 4 is almost exactly the same as the 2.5s in the fieros. You should have no problems bolting it up. Trans bolt pattern is the same.
Z||000002||ChadMan||07-27-99||07:14 AM||||I'm pretty sure the 87 2.5L 4 has a different intake manifold and TBI system than the 85.  If your 85's TBI and intake will bolt up to the 87 head (and I think it will), you'll be good to go.<p>If not, it'll be tougher...you'll have to use the Grand Am's TBI and manifold, which will necessitate rerouting of fuel lines and throttle cable.  In this case you would probably need an ECM and wiring harness from an 87 or 88 3-sp auto 4-cyl Fiero, too.  Good luck!
Z||000003||theogre||07-27-99||07:40 AM||||the 87+ motors use DIS distributorless electronic ignition. That motor Will Not drop into a pre 87 car without taking the ECM and harness. 
Z||000004||batboy||07-27-99||05:46 PM||||As Chad and Ogre have mentioned, the 1987 2.5 liter is not a direct swap into earlier Fieros. It will bolt onto the tranny, but you must use the donor car ECM and wiring harness like they said. I have a 87 Fiero with a dead motor. I'm having a terrible time locating another engine for it because the 87 motor was unique. I can find all kinds of 84 to 86 engines. I don't suppose you'd want to trade a good early 2.5 for that Grand Am would you? By the way, I'm being serious, assuming that engine you have is in running condition.
Z||000005||theogre||07-27-99||08:18 PM||||Bat,<p>Olds Cutlass, Pontiac 6000, and Grand Am, all use the same motor. You'll have to change all the brakets and water pump pully, but they will fit, and run with the ECM. Not all of the cast 4's 88+ where the balancer motors either. dig around. A balancer motor will work, but you'll be stuck with it's discusting pan filter. Any cast iron DIS 4 from 87 thru I think 91-92 should be a drop in. 87-89 for sure are.
Z||000006||batboy||07-27-99||09:01 PM||||Ogre, thanks, I'll use the 88 and newer engine if I have to, but still hoping to find a 87 one. Four Star remanufactured engine company is pretty close to where I live too, they have those 87 2.5 engines in stock, but I'm hoping to find something cheaper. Shhh, don't tell Sluppy, but I figured if I could con him out of that Grand Am, I'd have an engine, 5 speed tranny, brakes and all kinds of other parts that would come in handy. Besides, he only lives a few hours drive away and I have a flat bed trailer.
Z||000007||ChadMan||07-28-99||07:04 AM||||I have seen some 87's with DIS, and some without, and I have seen some 87's with the balance shafts and in-pan oil filter, and some without.  Freakin' GM.<p>A few years back I went to look at an 86 GT and was amazed to find that it had a 5-speed Getrag.  The poor guy had to show me his title before I would believe it wasn't an 87.  I guess GM changes componentry whenever they feel like it!
Z||000008||DJRice||07-28-99||08:30 AM||||Im having a heck-of-a-time on my engine swap, And I was going from fiero to fiero.  85 4 cyl in an 84 car.  I switched the cars wiring so I could take adantage of the different gauges, and she runs, but Im having problems.<p>I had the AC fixed last summer and upgraded to R-134, so I was very careful to leave the compressor in the car, not break any hoses or anything, but When I started the engine and turned on the AC it did not cool the air.  The clutch did not appear to be engaging.  When I turned the engine off, one of the hi pressure hoses going to the compressor blew up.  That was a mess.  I wouldnt bother with the 84, because I also have an 87, but I put a lot of work into that car last summer (brakes, AC, interior) and then the engine quit.  <p>Good luck on your swap.
Z||000009||sluppy123||07-29-99||06:57 PM||||Batboy-do you have a pre 87 4-cyl? well I just might have to trade you. But that would mean I wouldn't have this unique motor, better front brakes, five speed tranny, ect...  Well on second thought maybe I'll keep it.<p>Depending on the condition of your engine I would seriously consider trading. I don't have time to deal with a ECM, and wire swap. However the Grand-AM engine Isn't in the greatest condition, head needs some work, and it has a small oil leak. 
Z||000010||batboy||07-29-99||08:19 PM||||Sluppy, I tried to email you, but the message got returned. Please email me, if you would.
Z||000011||lowCG||07-29-99||11:36 PM||||GM changes everything,all the time,makes it hard to compare stuff when the recipe is never the same.This is true for cams,spring rates,ecm settings,they're like us,they like to f#&* around with stuff.When they order/make a run of parts,then change the specs,they usually use up the old parts first.
